From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Victor <victor@local>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 13:40:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] tp: 0019 probe GGUF capability flags in dist rank load

CORRECTNESS FIX. The distributed path produced fluent-looking but wrong output
on every prompt -- it failed a trivial repeating-token induction test -- because
`KimiK3DistRank::opt` (a `K3PlanOptions`, kimi_k3_dist_rank.h:18) was NEVER
ASSIGNED. Every flag in that struct defaults to false, and this file only ever
read it: passing it to `kimi_k3_load_weights_scoped` and copying it to
`out->fwd.opt`. Note the function's own `opt` PARAMETER is a different type
(`KimiK3DistRankLoadOptions`, carrying only verbose/max_ctx/policy/load_embed),
which is what makes the omission easy to miss on a read-through.

`kimi_k3_load_weights_scoped` gates tensor uploads on those flags
(kimi_k3.cpp:671-679), so weights that are PRESENT in the GGUF were never
loaded and the ops that consume them were skipped entirely:

  - shared experts   (ffn_gate_shexp / ffn_up_shexp / ffn_down_shexp)
  - routed RMSNorm   (ffn_routed_norm)
  - MLA q-lora       (attn_q_a / attn_q_b)
  - MLA attn gate    (attn_gate)

Nothing faults. Nothing is out of range. The affected layers just compute a
wrong answer, which is why 93 layers of "working" engine shipped on top of it.

On the real Kimi-K3 IQ1_S GGUF all four are present and all four were off:

    [k3-dist] caps probe(blk.3): q_lora=1 attn_gate=1 shexp=1 routed_norm=1

Localized by layer-by-layer bisection of the residual stream against an
external oracle -- llama.cpp PR #26185 (commit 4158193ed, build b10381), the
reference Kimi-K3 implementation -- fed byte-identical token ids:

  - layer 0 matched at cos 0.999999; layer 1 was the FIRST divergence at
    cos 0.874. This was at prompt position 0, a single token with no KV and no
    recurrent state, which rules out any state/indexing explanation.
  - layer 0 is the only dense-FFN layer (leading_dense_block_count=1) and
    layers 0,1,2 are all KDA (head_count_kv=[0,0,0,1,...]), so layer 0 vs 1
    differ in exactly one thing: dense FFN vs latent MoE.
  - router logits, top-16 routing weights and the post-allreduce routed-expert
    latent all matched at cos >= 0.9998 -- routing and expert math were never
    wrong.
  - two bitwise proofs of the skipped ops: the routed-norm input and output
    dumps were MD5-identical (norm skipped), and ffn_out was MD5-identical to
    the routed up-projection (shared expert never added).

Fix: probe the GGUF for the optional tensors and set the flags, exactly as
every `runtime/examples/*` harness already does (see kimi_k3_generate.cpp:109,
kimi_k3_smoke_test.cpp:49, kimi_k3_tp_check.cpp:120 and 16 others). Only the
production distributed path omitted it -- which is precisely why no
SparkInfer-vs-SparkInfer test could ever catch it: the harnesses set the flags
correctly and the engine under test did not.

blk.3 is the probe layer because blk.0 is the leading DENSE layer and has no
MoE tensors at all; probing it would false-negative shexp and routed_norm.

The resolved flags are printed once per rank at load so a run's logs record
what it actually ran rather than what the file happened to contain.

Verified after the fix, same oracle, same token ids:
  - all 93 layers match the reference at cos >= 0.99987 at prompt position 0
  - the induction test returns the oracle's exact next token (was a repeated
    junk token)
  - "The capital of France is" now decodes to " Paris. Paris is the capital of
    France." -- coherent, correct

NOTE this supersedes the root-cause theory recorded in 0016's commit message,
which attributed the collapse-to-one-repeated-token failure to IQ1_S
quantization plus greedy decoding. That was wrong: the collapse was this bug.
0016's repetition guard is still worth keeping as a decode-loop safety net, but
it was treating a symptom.

KNOWN GAP, not addressed here: at prompt positions > 0 the match against the
reference is looser (cosine dipping to ~0.79-0.86 in a band around layers
51-80, recovering by ~90-92). Suspected near-tied top-16 expert selection
flipping under IQ1_S quantization noise. NOT confirmed. Output is coherent and
first-token-exact; it is not verified byte-identical beyond that.
---
 runtime/src/models/kimi_k3_dist_rank.cpp | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/runtime/src/models/kimi_k3_dist_rank.cpp b/runtime/src/models/kimi_k3_dist_rank.cpp
--- a/runtime/src/models/kimi_k3_dist_rank.cpp
+++ b/runtime/src/models/kimi_k3_dist_rank.cpp
@@ -104,6 +104,26 @@ bool kimi_k3_dist_rank_load(const std::string& gguf_first_shard,
     const bool load_head = (spec.rank == 0);
     const bool load_embed = opt.load_embed_all_ranks || (spec.rank == 0);

+    // Capability flags are a property of the FILE and must be probed from it.
+    // They were never set here, so every one defaulted to false and the loader
+    // silently skipped weights that are present in the GGUF: the shared experts,
+    // the routed norm, and the MLA q-lora / attn-gate paths. Nothing faults --
+    // the affected layers just compute a wrong answer. Every runtime/examples/*
+    // harness does this probe, which is why none of them reproduced the fault.
+    {
+        const int probe = out->cfg.n_layers > 3 ? 3 : 0;
+        const std::string p = "blk." + std::to_string(probe) + ".";
+        out->opt.has_q_lora         = g.tensor((p + "attn_q_a.weight").c_str()) != nullptr;
+        out->opt.has_attn_gate      = g.tensor((p + "attn_gate.weight").c_str()) != nullptr;
+        out->opt.has_fused_kv_b     = false;
+        out->opt.has_shared_experts = g.tensor((p + "ffn_gate_shexp.weight").c_str()) != nullptr;
+        out->opt.has_routed_norm    = g.tensor((p + "ffn_routed_norm.weight").c_str()) != nullptr;
+        std::fprintf(stderr,
+                     "[k3-dist] caps probe(blk.%d): q_lora=%d attn_gate=%d shexp=%d routed_norm=%d\n",
+                     probe, (int)out->opt.has_q_lora, (int)out->opt.has_attn_gate,
+                     (int)out->opt.has_shared_experts, (int)out->opt.has_routed_norm);
+    }
+
     if (!kimi_k3_load_weights_scoped(g, out->cfg, out->opt, out->weights,
                                      /*first_layer=*/0,
                                      /*last_layer=*/out->cfg.n_layers - 1, load_embed,
--
2.43.0
